opressin is responsible for  telling the kidneys to reabsorb water  back into the body after it filters out  toxins in the blood however with  vasopressin blocked the kidneys are not  told to reabsorb water  and as a result the filtered water goes  directly to the bladder this explains  why you pee more frequently after  drinking now the organ you've been  waiting for the liver ethanol in the  liver are the oldest rivals in the book  the liver detoxifies about 90 of the  ethanol that you drink into harmless  acetic acid which is then converted into  carbon dioxide and water however ethanol  being the bear that it is doesn't go  down without a fight  before it becomes acetic acid it's  converted into an intermediate  metabolite aceta aldehyde which is very  toxic to the liver acetaldehyde  denatures proteins and liver cells  causing inflammation and apoptosis the  liver is a robust organ and is made to  handle this kind of stuff so the  occasional drink even moderate drinking  is okay but with chronic and heavy  drinking the liver takes a beating from  constant exposure to inflammation in  response to inflammatory injury the  liver grows fibrous connective tissue  known as fibrosis fibrotic tissue is  rigid and non-functional so whatever  tissue becomes fibrotic can no longer  detoxify ethanol or anything toxic in  the body this is known as cirrhosis of  the liver with cirrhosis the body  retains large concentrations of salt in  the abdomen causing an accumulation of  fluid known as edema this leads to the  appearance of a bloated abdomen which is  a telltale sign of a heavy drinker  continued heavy drinking causes  cirrhosis to progress to the point that  the liver is unable to detoxify anything  known as liver failure now the liver is  not the only organ that detoxifies  ethanol if you recall the liver handles  about 90 percent of the ethanol we drink  but what about the remaining 10 percent  oddly enough the pancreas does detoxify  ethanol as well just like the liver the  pancreas converts ethanol into  acetaldehyde and then acetic acid the  pancreas is also injured by inflammation  caused by acetaldehyde however it has a  much lower tolerance to injury than the  liver for moderate drinking the pancreas  is fine it metabolizes a small amount of  alcohol so you're generally okay but  with chronic drinking it can be deadly  the pancreas holds digestive enzymes  that are sent to the intestines for food  breakdown the organ sits behind the  stomach and excretes enzymes into the  beginning of the small intestines these  enzymes are stored in the pancreas and  are not activated until they're released  however the effects of severe  inflammation from acetaldehyde can cause  these inactive enzymes to become  activated and actually digest the  pancreas from the inside out this  persistent injury leads to alcoholic  pancreatitis which is notably painful  what's worse is pancreatitis increases  the risk for pancreatic cancer which is  almost a death sentence and lastly the  final item to the list of the many  things that alcohol does to your body is  it affects your bone i'm telling you  alcohol is the worst thing for you oh  sorry one second  first off light to moderate drinking has  not been shown to adversely affect your  bones so you're okay  not surprisingly though excessive  long-term drinking has been shown to  decrease bone density this more so has  to do with your liver though your bones  are affected down the line if your liver  is in the beginning stages of liver  disease because you drink too much for  far too long its functions begin to drop  in performance one important thing the  liver does aside from detoxification is  it converts vitamin d into its active  metabolite for the body to use to absorb  calcium if your liver is not working the  way it's supposed to you're not  activating enough vitamin d and you're  unable to absorb calcium over time your  bones begin to lose their structural  integrity known as  osteoporosis and that's everything that  alcohol does to your body quick note  this video was not an anti-alcohol video  in case you didn't notice i made the  point several times during the video  that i enjoy drinking alcohol the key  takeaway here is that alcohol really is  not good for you to drink but it's okay  if you drink it in moderation it's only  when you abuse it and you drink it  chronically for many years that you fall  into problems so don't do that so i  shouldn't see any ambiguity or bickering  in the comments okay awesome so  producers we're good here right we're  good to wrap up okay thank you and  writers this this is all good cool and  uh hr is this is this good  no  okay i'll get back to you next week all  right guys that's the video thanks for  watching i'll catch you next time later

